        "code": {
          "text": "Globus pharyngeus / non-specific throat sensation, likely functional due to stress and voice strain; possible reflux component"
        },
        "clinicalStatus": {
          "coding": [
            {
              "system": "http://terminology.hl7.org/CodeSystem/condition-clinical",
              "code": "active"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Condition/condition-2",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Condition",
        "id": "condition-2",
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "code": {
          "text": "Premenstrual syndrome, moderate, predominantly mood and irritability symptoms"
        },
        "clinicalStatus": {
          "coding": [
            {
              "system": "http://terminology.hl7.org/CodeSystem/condition-clinical",
              "code": "active"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-1",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-1",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Throat symptoms"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Intermittent tight or stuck sensation in the throat, especially in the evenings when lying down, reported as a 4–5/7 history. No true pain, dysphagia to solids or fluids, odynophagia, choking, or regurgitation. Mild intermittent hoarseness, especially after a full day of teaching or yelling. No coryzal symptoms, cough, fevers, weight loss, or night sweats. Similar globus episodes during stress in the past."
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-2",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-2",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Premenstrual symptoms"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Longstanding premenstrual irritability, marked mood swings, bloating, breast tenderness, and headaches in the week before menses; symptoms settle 1–2 days after bleeding starts, with the patient essentially well mid-cycle. Cycles regular, 28–30 days; bleeding lasts 5–6 days. A symptom diary was only partly completed for one cycle; the recorded pattern was luteal-phase symptoms resolving after menses. Physical symptoms are milder than mood symptoms and irritability."
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-3",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-3",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Mood symptoms"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "In the week before the period, feels like she is going to snap at everyone, with increased conflict at work and with her partner. Reports being somewhat more short-fused through the month since starting a new class. No persistent low mood or anhedonia outside the premenstrual week."
      }
    },
    {
